i><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikandar-lodi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikandar Lodi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sikandar Lodi was a sultan of Delhi of the Lodi dynasty, who ruled in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-shah-bengal"><span class="enc-name">Sikandar Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sikandar Shah was sultan of Bengal from 1358 to about 1390, son of Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikasso"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikasso.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikasso</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sikasso is a city in southern Mali, near the borders with Burkina Faso and Côte d'Ivoire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy"><span class="enc-name">Sikh Confederacy</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sikh Confederacy refers to the group of Sikh misls, or confederate clans, that governed the Punjab after the decline of Mughal authority in the eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikh-conquest-of-kashmir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikh-conquest-of-kashmir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikh conquest of Kashmir</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · 1819</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sikh conquest of Kashmir took place in 1819, when the forces of Ranjit Singh took the valley from its Afghan rulers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikh-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikh Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sikh Empire was a state in the Punjab founded by Maharaja Ranjit Singh at the start of the nineteenth century, with its capital at Lahore.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikh-gurus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikh gurus</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sikh gurus were the ten spiritual masters who founded and led Sikhism, from Guru Nanak, born in 1469, to Guru Gobind Singh, who died in 1708.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikhism"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikhism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikhism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Sikhism is a monotheistic religion founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sikhs"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sikhs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sikhs</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sikhs are followers of Sikhism, a monotheistic religion founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab in the early 16th century and shaped by nine succeeding Gurus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siksika"><span class="enc-name">Siksika</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siksika, or Blackfoot proper, are one of the nations of the Blackfoot Confederacy of the northern Great Plains, together with the Kainai and the Piikani.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siku-quanshu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siku-quanshu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siku Quanshu</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siku Quanshu, or Complete Library of the Four Treasuries, was a vast compilation of Chinese literature ordered by the Qianlong Emperor in 1773 and completed in 1782.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sil-muiredaig"><span class="enc-name">Síl Muiredaig</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Síl Muiredaig were the ruling kindred of Connacht from the 8th century, descended from Muiredach Muillethan, a king of Connacht who died in 702.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sil-naedo-slaine"><span class="enc-name">Síl nÁedo Sláine</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Síl nÁedo Sláine was a branch of the southern Uí Néill dynasty of early medieval Ireland, descended from Áed Sláine, a 7th-century king of Tara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sila-calabria"><span class="enc-name">Sila (Calabria)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sila is a high forested plateau in Calabria, in the toe of southern Italy, rising to about 1,900 metres.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sila-region"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sila-region.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sila Region</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sila is a region of south-eastern Chad, on the border with Sudan, with its capital at Goz Beïda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/silahara-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height=